var e={domain:`frontend`,slug:`react-internals`,title:`React rendering internals`,order:4,summary:`Reconciliation, Fiber, render vs commit and concurrent rendering, the "how does React actually work" round.`,questions:[{id:`ri-virtual-dom`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`Virtual DOM vs real DOM: is the virtual DOM "faster"?`,difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`virtual-dom`,`rendering`],explain:`Not by itself, because nothing beats a perfectly targeted manual DOM update. The virtual DOM is a lightweight JavaScript description of the UI. It lets you write **declarative** code (describe the result, not the steps) while React works out the minimal real DOM changes and batches them.`,hook:`📝 The virtual DOM is a draft you can scribble on freely; React only copies the differences onto the real page.`,senior:`The win is predictable, batched updates without hand-written DOM code, not raw speed. Frameworks like Svelte and Solid skip the virtual DOM entirely.`,html:{explain:`<p>Not by itself, because nothing beats a perfectly targeted manual DOM update. The virtual DOM is a lightweight JavaScript description of the UI. It lets you write <strong>declarative</strong> code (describe the result, not the steps) while React works out the minimal real DOM changes and batches them.</p>`,hook:`<p>📝 The virtual DOM is a draft you can scribble on freely; React only copies the differences onto the real page.</p>`,senior:`<p>The win is predictable, batched updates without hand-written DOM code, not raw speed. Frameworks like Svelte and Solid skip the virtual DOM entirely.</p>`}},{id:`ri-jsx`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`What is JSX, and what does it compile to?`,difficulty:`easy`,tags:[`jsx`],explain:"JSX is syntax that looks like HTML inside JavaScript. It compiles to function calls that create element objects (`jsx(type, props)`, formerly `React.createElement`). It exists so you don't write those nested calls by hand.",hook:`🍬 JSX is sugar: sweet to write, but underneath it's just function calls returning plain objects.`,example:`\`\`\`jsx
<div className="card">{text}</div>
// compiles to roughly:
jsx('div', { className: 'card', children: text })
\`\`\``,html:{explain:`<p>JSX is syntax that looks like HTML inside JavaScript. It compiles to function calls that create element objects (<code>jsx(type, props)</code>, formerly <code>React.createElement</code>). It exists so you don't write those nested calls by hand.</p>`,hook:`<p>🍬 JSX is sugar: sweet to write, but underneath it's just function calls returning plain objects.</p>`,example:`<div class="codeblock"><pre tabindex="0"><code class="language-jsx hljs">&#x3C;div className=<span class="hljs-string">"card"</span>>{text}&#x3C;/div>
<span class="hljs-comment">// compiles to roughly:</span>
<span class="hljs-title function_">jsx</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'div'</span>, { <span class="hljs-attr">className</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'card'</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">children</span>: text })
</code></pre><button type="button" class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button></div>`}},{id:`ri-rerender-triggers`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`What triggers a re-render?`,difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`rendering`,`state`],explain:"Three things: a state update in that component (`useState`/`useReducer`), its **parent re-rendering**, or a context it consumes changing. \"Props changed\" isn't a separate trigger, since props change *because* the parent re-rendered. By default a parent's re-render renders **all** its children unless they're memoised.",hook:`🔔 Three bells ring a re-render: my own state, my parent, my context.`,html:{explain:`<p>Three things: a state update in that component (<code>useState</code>/<code>useReducer</code>), its <strong>parent re-rendering</strong>, or a context it consumes changing. "Props changed" isn't a separate trigger, since props change <em>because</em> the parent re-rendered. By default a parent's re-render renders <strong>all</strong> its children unless they're memoised.</p>`,hook:`<p>🔔 Three bells ring a re-render: my own state, my parent, my context.</p>`}},{id:`ri-render-vs-commit`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`What are the render phase and the commit phase?`,difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`rendering`,`effects`],demo:`react-render-commit`,explain:"**Render:** React calls your components and diffs the result against the previous tree. It must be pure, and it can be paused, restarted, thrown away or run more than once. **Commit:** React applies the DOM changes in one go, attaches refs, runs `useLayoutEffect` synchronously, lets the browser paint, then runs `useEffect`. That's why side effects never go in the render body.",hook:`✍️ Render is writing the plan in pencil (erasable); commit is pouring the concrete (once, all at once).`,html:{explain:`<p><strong>Render:</strong> React calls your components and diffs the result against the previous tree. It must be pure, and it can be paused, restarted, thrown away or run more than once. <strong>Commit:</strong> React applies the DOM changes in one go, attaches refs, runs <code>useLayoutEffect</code> synchronously, lets the browser paint, then runs <code>useEffect</code>. That's why side effects never go in the render body.</p>`,hook:`<p>✍️ Render is writing the plan in pencil (erasable); commit is pouring the concrete (once, all at once).</p>`}},{id:`ri-reconciliation`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`How does reconciliation (the diffing algorithm) work?`,difficulty:`hard`,tags:[`reconciliation`,`rendering`],explain:"A general tree diff is O(n³), so React uses two rules to make it O(n). **Different element type at the same position** (`div` → `span`, `A` → `B`): tear down the whole subtree and build a new one, losing state. **Same type**: keep the DOM node or component instance and update only the changed props. For lists of children, **keys** match items between renders.",hook:`🔁 Same type, same spot = renovate. Different type = demolish and rebuild.`,html:{explain:`<p>A general tree diff is O(n³), so React uses two rules to make it O(n). <strong>Different element type at the same position</strong> (<code>div</code> → <code>span</code>, <code>A</code> → <code>B</code>): tear down the whole subtree and build a new one, losing state. <strong>Same type</strong>: keep the DOM node or component instance and update only the changed props. For lists of children, <strong>keys</strong> match items between renders.</p>`,hook:`<p>🔁 Same type, same spot = renovate. Different type = demolish and rebuild.</p>`}},{id:`ri-keys`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`How do keys work, and why not use the array index?`,difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`reconciliation`,`lists`],explain:`Without keys React matches children by position, so inserting at the top makes every item look changed. With keys it matches by identity: it moves existing nodes, creates new ones and removes missing ones. Keys must be stable and unique among siblings. Index keys break on reorder, insert or delete, because state and DOM get attached to the wrong item.`,hook:`🏷️ A key is a name tag; an index is a seat number, and seats change when someone new sits down.`,example:"```jsx\n{todos.map(t => <TodoItem key={t.id} todo={t} />)}   // stable identity\n<Profile key={userId} />  // trick: changing the key forces a full remount (resets state)\n```",html:{explain:`<p>Without keys React matches children by position, so inserting at the top makes every item look changed. With keys it matches by identity: it moves existing nodes, creates new ones and removes missing ones. Keys must be stable and unique among siblings. Index keys break on reorder, insert or delete, because state and DOM get attached to the wrong item.</p>`,hook:`<p>🏷️ A key is a name tag; an index is a seat number, and seats change when someone new sits down.</p>`,example:`<div class="codeblock"><pre tabindex="0"><code class="language-jsx hljs">{todos.<span class="hljs-title function_">map</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">t</span> =></span> <span class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;<span class="hljs-name">TodoItem</span> <span class="hljs-attr">key</span>=<span class="hljs-string">{t.id}</span> <span class="hljs-attr">todo</span>=<span class="hljs-string">{t}</span> /></span></span>)}   <span class="hljs-comment">// stable identity</span>
&#x3C;<span class="hljs-title class_">Profile</span> key={userId} />  <span class="hljs-comment">// trick: changing the key forces a full remount (resets state)</span>
</code></pre><button type="button" class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button></div>`}},{id:`ri-state-preservation`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`When does a component keep its state, and when does it lose it?`,difficulty:`hard`,tags:[`state`,`reconciliation`],explain:"State lives at a **position in the tree**. The same type at the same position keeps it, even across a ternary (`{a ? <Counter/> : <Counter/>}` shares one state). A different type, a different key, or the component moving position resets it. Classic bug: defining a component *inside* another component creates a new type every render, so it remounts and loses state every time.",hook:`📍 State is tied to the seat, not the person: same seat, same state; new seat or new type, fresh start.`,example:`\`\`\`jsx
function Parent() {
  // ❌ new component type every render → Child remounts, input loses focus
  const Child = () => <input />;
  return <Child />;
}
\`\`\``,html:{explain:`<p>State lives at a <strong>position in the tree</strong>. The same type at the same position keeps it, even across a ternary (<code>{a ? &#x3C;Counter/> : &#x3C;Counter/>}</code> shares one state). A different type, a different key, or the component moving position resets it. Classic bug: defining a component <em>inside</em> another component creates a new type every render, so it remounts and loses state every time.</p>`,hook:`<p>📍 State is tied to the seat, not the person: same seat, same state; new seat or new type, fresh start.</p>`,example:`<div class="codeblock"><pre tabindex="0"><code class="language-jsx hljs"><span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">Parent</span>(<span class="hljs-params"></span>) {
  <span class="hljs-comment">// ❌ new component type every render → Child remounts, input loses focus</span>
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">Child</span> = (<span class="hljs-params"></span>) => <span class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;<span class="hljs-name">input</span> /></span></span>;
  <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;<span class="hljs-name">Child</span> /></span></span>;
}
</code></pre><button type="button" class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button></div>`}},{id:`ri-fiber`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`What is Fiber?`,difficulty:`hard`,tags:[`fiber`,`concurrency`],explain:`React's reconciliation engine since React 16. A **fiber** is a unit of work, one per component or element, linked by child, sibling and parent pointers, so React walks the tree in a loop instead of recursion. That lets it **pause, resume, prioritise or abandon** rendering. React keeps two trees, *current* (on screen) and *work-in-progress*, and swaps them on commit.`,hook:`🧶 Old React knitted the whole scarf in one go; Fiber knits a row, checks if you need it to stop, then carries on.`,senior:`Before Fiber, reconciliation was synchronous and recursive, so a big update blocked the main thread and dropped frames. Fiber is the foundation that Suspense and transitions are built on.`,html:{explain:`<p>React's reconciliation engine since React 16. A <strong>fiber</strong> is a unit of work, one per component or element, linked by child, sibling and parent pointers, so React walks the tree in a loop instead of recursion. That lets it <strong>pause, resume, prioritise or abandon</strong> rendering. React keeps two trees, <em>current</em> (on screen) and <em>work-in-progress</em>, and swaps them on commit.</p>`,hook:`<p>🧶 Old React knitted the whole scarf in one go; Fiber knits a row, checks if you need it to stop, then carries on.</p>`,senior:`<p>Before Fiber, reconciliation was synchronous and recursive, so a big update blocked the main thread and dropped frames. Fiber is the foundation that Suspense and transitions are built on.</p>`}},{id:`ri-concurrent-rendering`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`What does concurrent rendering actually do?`,difficulty:`hard`,tags:[`concurrency`,`performance`],explain:`Rendering becomes interruptible. React works in small slices (about 5ms), yields to the browser so input and paint stay smooth, and can drop a stale render if newer state arrives. Urgent updates (typing) take priority over transitions (filtering a big list). The commit phase is still synchronous, so you never see a half-finished UI.`,hook:`🚑 Concurrent React lets the ambulance (your keystroke) overtake the delivery truck (the big re-render).`,html:{explain:`<p>Rendering becomes interruptible. React works in small slices (about 5ms), yields to the browser so input and paint stay smooth, and can drop a stale render if newer state arrives. Urgent updates (typing) take priority over transitions (filtering a big list). The commit phase is still synchronous, so you never see a half-finished UI.</p>`,hook:`<p>🚑 Concurrent React lets the ambulance (your keystroke) overtake the delivery truck (the big re-render).</p>`}},{id:`ri-tearing`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`What is "tearing", and how does React prevent it?`,difficulty:`hard`,tags:[`concurrency`,`external-store`],explain:"During an interruptible render, an external store can change midway, so different components show different versions of the same data. `useSyncExternalStore` prevents it by forcing a consistent snapshot of the store for the whole render.",hook:`📸 Tearing is half the class photo taken before the teacher moved and half after.`,example:`\`\`\`js
const online = useSyncExternalStore(
  (cb) => { addEventListener('online', cb); addEventListener('offline', cb);
            return () => { removeEventListener('online', cb); removeEventListener('offline', cb); }; },
  () => navigator.onLine,
);
\`\`\``,html:{explain:`<p>During an interruptible render, an external store can change midway, so different components show different versions of the same data. <code>useSyncExternalStore</code> prevents it by forcing a consistent snapshot of the store for the whole render.</p>`,hook:`<p>📸 Tearing is half the class photo taken before the teacher moved and half after.</p>`,example:`<div class="codeblock"><pre tabindex="0"><code class="language-js hljs"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> online = <span class="hljs-title function_">useSyncExternalStore</span>(
  <span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">cb</span>) =></span> { <span class="hljs-title function_">addEventListener</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'online'</span>, cb); <span class="hljs-title function_">addEventListener</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'offline'</span>, cb);
            <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-function">() =></span> { <span class="hljs-title function_">removeEventListener</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'online'</span>, cb); <span class="hljs-title function_">removeEventListener</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'offline'</span>, cb); }; },
  <span class="hljs-function">() =></span> navigator.<span class="hljs-property">onLine</span>,
);
</code></pre><button type="button" class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button></div>`}},{id:`ri-setstate-batching`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:"Why does `setState` seem asynchronous?",difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`state`,`batching`],explain:"Updates are queued and **batched**, and the state you read during a render is a **snapshot** for that render. So calling `setCount(count + 1)` twice adds 1, not 2. Use the updater form `setCount(c => c + 1)` when the next state depends on the previous one. `flushSync` forces an immediate update but is rarely needed.",hook:`📮 setState drops a letter in the post box; you won't see the reply until the next render opens the mail.`,example:"```js\nsetCount(count + 1); setCount(count + 1); // +1 (same snapshot)\nsetCount(c => c + 1); setCount(c => c + 1); // +2 (each sees the latest)\n```",html:{explain:`<p>Updates are queued and <strong>batched</strong>, and the state you read during a render is a <strong>snapshot</strong> for that render. So calling <code>setCount(count + 1)</code> twice adds 1, not 2. Use the updater form <code>setCount(c => c + 1)</code> when the next state depends on the previous one. <code>flushSync</code> forces an immediate update but is rarely needed.</p>`,hook:`<p>📮 setState drops a letter in the post box; you won't see the reply until the next render opens the mail.</p>`,example:`<div class="codeblock"><pre tabindex="0"><code class="language-js hljs"><span class="hljs-title function_">setCount</span>(count + <span class="hljs-number">1</span>); <span class="hljs-title function_">setCount</span>(count + <span class="hljs-number">1</span>); <span class="hljs-comment">// +1 (same snapshot)</span>
<span class="hljs-title function_">setCount</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">c</span> =></span> c + <span class="hljs-number">1</span>); <span class="hljs-title function_">setCount</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">c</span> =></span> c + <span class="hljs-number">1</span>); <span class="hljs-comment">// +2 (each sees the latest)</span>
</code></pre><button type="button" class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button></div>`}},{id:`ri-effect-order`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`What's the order of effects?`,difficulty:`hard`,tags:[`effects`,`lifecycle`],explain:"In the commit phase: DOM mutations, then refs attached, then **layout effects** (children before parents), then the browser paints, then **passive effects** (`useEffect`, children before parents). On re-run or unmount, the previous cleanup runs before the new effect.",hook:`👶 Kids before parents, layout before paint, paint before passive effects, and always clean up before you set up again.`,html:{explain:`<p>In the commit phase: DOM mutations, then refs attached, then <strong>layout effects</strong> (children before parents), then the browser paints, then <strong>passive effects</strong> (<code>useEffect</code>, children before parents). On re-run or unmount, the previous cleanup runs before the new effect.</p>`,hook:`<p>👶 Kids before parents, layout before paint, paint before passive effects, and always clean up before you set up again.</p>`}},{id:`ri-synthetic-events`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`How does React handle events?`,difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`events`],explain:"React uses **event delegation**: one listener per event type on the root container (since React 17; on `document` before that), which dispatches **synthetic events**, a cross-browser wrapper over native events. Updates triggered inside one event handler are batched into one render.",hook:`☎️ One receptionist at the front desk (the root) takes every call and forwards it to the right component.`,html:{explain:`<p>React uses <strong>event delegation</strong>: one listener per event type on the root container (since React 17; on <code>document</code> before that), which dispatches <strong>synthetic events</strong>, a cross-browser wrapper over native events. Updates triggered inside one event handler are batched into one render.</p>`,hook:`<p>☎️ One receptionist at the front desk (the root) takes every call and forwards it to the right component.</p>`}},{id:`ri-hydration`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`Explain hydration in terms of the DOM.`,difficulty:`hard`,tags:[`ssr`,`hydration`],explain:"Server rendering sends real HTML, so users see content before any JavaScript runs. Hydration then runs the components on the client and **attaches** to the existing DOM instead of recreating it, wiring up events and state. If the client's first render differs from the server HTML, you get a hydration mismatch. React 18's **selective hydration** lets `Suspense` boundaries hydrate independently, prioritising whichever one the user interacts with first.",hook:`💧 SSR delivers the dry sponge (HTML); hydration adds water (events and state) without reshaping it.`,html:{explain:`<p>Server rendering sends real HTML, so users see content before any JavaScript runs. Hydration then runs the components on the client and <strong>attaches</strong> to the existing DOM instead of recreating it, wiring up events and state. If the client's first render differs from the server HTML, you get a hydration mismatch. React 18's <strong>selective hydration</strong> lets <code>Suspense</code> boundaries hydrate independently, prioritising whichever one the user interacts with first.</p>`,hook:`<p>💧 SSR delivers the dry sponge (HTML); hydration adds water (events and state) without reshaping it.</p>`}},{id:`ri-rsc-payload`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`What's the difference between the RSC payload and SSR HTML?`,difficulty:`hard`,tags:[`server-components`,`ssr`],explain:`SSR HTML is for the first paint. The RSC payload is a serialised description of the Server Component tree (with placeholders for Client Components) that React uses to build and update the tree on the client. On client-side navigation, Next.js fetches only the RSC payload, not HTML.`,hook:`🧾 HTML is the printed page; the RSC payload is the editable blueprint React keeps updating from.`,html:{explain:`<p>SSR HTML is for the first paint. The RSC payload is a serialised description of the Server Component tree (with placeholders for Client Components) that React uses to build and update the tree on the client. On client-side navigation, Next.js fetches only the RSC payload, not HTML.</p>`,hook:`<p>🧾 HTML is the printed page; the RSC payload is the editable blueprint React keeps updating from.</p>`}},{id:`ri-avoid-rerenders`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`How do you stop unnecessary re-renders, in order of preference?`,difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`performance`,`memoisation`],explain:"First, move state down, closer to where it's used. Second, lift content up and pass it as `children`, because children created by the parent don't re-render when the wrapper's state changes. Third, split contexts. Last, `memo` with stable props (`useMemo`/`useCallback`, or the React Compiler). Measure with the Profiler before and after.",hook:`⬇️ Move state down, lift content up, split context, memo last.`,example:"```jsx\n// The slow tree is created by the parent, so typing in <Panel> doesn't re-render it\n<Panel>\n  <ExpensiveTree />\n</Panel>\n```",html:{explain:`<p>First, move state down, closer to where it's used. Second, lift content up and pass it as <code>children</code>, because children created by the parent don't re-render when the wrapper's state changes. Third, split contexts. Last, <code>memo</code> with stable props (<code>useMemo</code>/<code>useCallback</code>, or the React Compiler). Measure with the Profiler before and after.</p>`,hook:`<p>⬇️ Move state down, lift content up, split context, memo last.</p>`,example:`<div class="codeblock"><pre tabindex="0"><code class="language-jsx hljs"><span class="hljs-comment">// The slow tree is created by the parent, so typing in &#x3C;Panel> doesn't re-render it</span>
&#x3C;<span class="hljs-title class_">Panel</span>>
  <span class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;<span class="hljs-name">ExpensiveTree</span> /></span></span>
&#x3C;/<span class="hljs-title class_">Panel</span>>
</code></pre><button type="button" class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button></div>`}}]};export{e as default};